---

<div class="home-premium-page">
  <!-- ===== 1. HERO SECTION ===== -->
  <section class="home-hero" aria-labelledby="home-title">
    <div class="home-hero__content">
      <p class="home-badge">FAST • ACCURATE • TRUSTED</p>
      <h1 id="home-title">Amps to Watts Calculator</h1>
      <p class="home-hero__lead">
        Use this amps to watts calculator to convert electrical current in amps
        into power in watts using voltage and, for AC circuits, power factor. It
        supports DC, single-phase AC, and three-phase AC calculations so you can
        estimate appliance loads, battery power, generator capacity, EV charging
        power, and electrical circuit demand.
      </p>
      <div class="home-trust-list" aria-label="Calculator benefits">
        <div>
          <span class="home-icon home-icon--blue" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              ><path d="M13 2 5 14h6l-1 8 9-13h-6l0-7Z"></path></svg
            >
          </span>
          <strong>DC, Single-Phase & Three-Phase</strong>
          <small>Supports all common current types in one tool</small>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="home-icon home-icon--blue" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              ><path
                d="M12 3 5 6v5c0 4.4 2.9 8.5 7 10 4.1-1.5 7-5.6 7-10V6l-7-3Zm3.4 7.4-4.1 4.1-2-2 1.4-1.4.6.6 2.7-2.7 1.4 1.4Z"
              ></path></svg
            >
          </span>
          <strong>Correct Formula for Each Type</strong>
          <small>Uses industry-standard formulas with power factor</small>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="home-icon home-icon--blue" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              ><path
                d="M12 4a8 8 0 1 0 8 8h-2a6 6 0 1 1-6-6V4Zm1 3h-2v6l5 3 1-1.7-4-2.3V7Z"
              ></path></svg
            >
          </span>
          <strong>Watts & Kilowatts Output</strong>
          <small>Results in both watts and kW for quick reference</small>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="home-icon home-icon--blue" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              ><path
                d="M19 3H5a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h14a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V5a2 2 0 0 0-2-2Zm-7 14-5-5 1.4-1.4L12 14.2l5.6-5.6L19 10l-7 7Z"
              ></path></svg
            >
          </span>
          <strong>Appliances, Solar, EVs & More</strong>
          <small>Helpful for batteries, generators, motors, and chargers</small>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="home-calculator-wrapper">
      <PremiumToolCalculator config={getCalculatorConfig("homepage")} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 2. HOW TO USE THE CALCULATOR ===== -->
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="how-to-use">
    <div class="home-section-card">
      <div class="home-section-heading">
        <h2 id="how-to-use">How to Use the Amps to Watts Calculator</h2>
        <p>Follow these steps to convert amps to watts for any circuit type.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-content-body">
        <p>
          To calculate watts from amps, choose the current type, enter the
          current in amps, enter the voltage, and add power factor for AC loads.
          The calculator shows the estimated power in watts and kilowatts.
        </p>
        <div class="home-field-guide">
          <div>
            <strong>Current type</strong>
            <span>Choose DC, single-phase AC, or three-phase AC depending on your circuit.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Current (amps)</strong>
            <span>Enter the load current in amps from the equipment nameplate or a clamp meter reading.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Voltage (volts)</strong>
            <span>Enter the circuit or device voltage. Common values: 12V, 24V, 120V, 208V, 240V, 277V, 480V.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Power factor</strong>
            <span>Use 1.0 for resistive loads (heaters, lights) or the nameplate value for motors and AC equipment. If unknown, 0.85 is a safe estimate.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Voltage type (three-phase)</strong>
            <span>For three-phase systems, choose line-to-line or line-to-neutral. Most industrial equipment uses line-to-line voltage.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 3. AMPS TO WATTS FORMULAS ===== -->
  <section class="home-section home-formulas" aria-labelledby="formulas">
    <div class="home-section-card">
      <div class="home-section-heading">
        <h2 id="formulas">Amps to Watts Formulas</h2>
        <p>The correct formula depends on your circuit type — DC, AC single-phase, or AC three-phase.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-grid">
        {
          formulaCards.map((card) => (
            <article
              class={`home-formula-card home-card-hover home-tone-${card.tone}`}
            >
              <div class="home-formula-top">
                <span>{card.badge}</span>
                <strong>{card.title}</strong>
              </div>
              <p class="home-math">{card.formula}</p>
              <p>{card.description}</p>
              <small>{card.example}</small>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="home-content-body home-formula-explanation">
        <p>
          DC circuits use amps and volts directly — power equals current times
          voltage. AC circuits often need power factor because not all apparent
          power becomes real power. Inductive loads like motors and compressors
          draw reactive current that does no useful work, which lowers the power
          factor below 1.0. Three-phase systems need a different multiplier
          because electrical power is distributed across three conductors with a
          120° phase offset.
        </p>
        <p class="home-formula-note">
          <strong>Note:</strong> These formulas calculate watts (real power), not
          watt-hours or kilowatt-hours. Watts measure instantaneous power consumption.
          To find energy usage over time, multiply watts by hours.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 4. DC AMPS TO WATTS ===== -->
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="dc-explanation">
    <div class="home-section-card">
      <div class="home-section-heading">
        <h2 id="dc-explanation">DC Amps to Watts</h2>
        <p>Direct current conversion is the simplest case.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-content-body">
        <p>
          In a DC circuit — such as a battery, solar panel, or automotive system —
          converting amps to watts requires only the current and voltage:
          <strong>Watts = Amps × Volts</strong>. There is no power factor in DC
          because the current and voltage waveforms do not alternate. A 12V
          battery delivering 5 amps produces exactly 60 watts of real power. This
          formula applies to all DC applications, from USB chargers at 5V to
          solar arrays at 48V and telecommunications systems at 24V or 48V.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use the <a href="/dc-amps-to-watts-calculator/">DC amps to watts calculator</a>
          for a dedicated tool pre-configured for direct current, or
          <a href="/12v-amps-to-watts/">12V</a>,
          <a href="/24v-amps-to-watts/">24V</a> voltage-specific calculators for
          common battery systems.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 5. AC SINGLE-PHASE AMPS TO WATTS ===== -->
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="ac-single-phase">
    <div class="home-section-card">
      <div class="home-section-heading">
        <h2 id="ac-single-phase">AC Single-Phase Amps to Watts</h2>
        <p>Most household and small commercial circuits are single-phase AC.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-content-body">
        <p>
          For AC single-phase circuits, the amps to watts formula includes power
          factor: <strong>Watts = Amps × Volts × Power Factor</strong>. Resistive
          loads like heaters, incandescent lights, and toasters have a power factor
          of 1.0, which means all the apparent power becomes real power. Inductive
          loads such as motors, compressors, and fluorescent ballasts have a lower
          power factor — typically between 0.70 and 0.95 — so their real power is
          less than the simple amps × volts product.
        </p>
        <p>
          For most standard household appliances on <a href="/120v-amps-to-watts/">120V</a>
          or <a href="/240v-amps-to-watts/">240V</a> circuits, the amps to watts
          conversion is straightforward. Use the
          <a href="/ac-amps-to-watts-calculator/">AC amps to watts calculator</a>
          or the <a href="/single-phase-amps-to-watts/">single-phase amps to watts</a>
          tool for pre-configured single-phase calculations.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 6. THREE-PHASE AMPS TO WATTS ===== -->
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="three-phase">
    <div class="home-section-card">
      <div class="home-section-heading">
        <h2 id="three-phase">Three-Phase Amps to Watts</h2>
        <p>Industrial and commercial power systems typically use three-phase AC.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-content-body">
        <p>
          Three-phase amps to watts conversion uses a different multiplier
          depending on whether you measure line-to-line or line-to-neutral
          voltage. For <strong>line-to-line voltage</strong> (the most common
          measurement): <strong>Watts = √3 × Amps × Volts × PF</strong>, where
          √3 ≈ 1.732. For <strong>line-to-neutral voltage</strong>:
          <strong>Watts = 3 × Amps × Volts × PF</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The √3 factor accounts for the 120° phase offset between the three
          power conductors. Common three-phase voltages include 208V, 400V,
          <a href="/480v-amps-to-watts-calculator/">480V</a>, and 600V
          (line-to-line). Use the
          <a href="/3-phase-amps-to-watts-calculator/">3-phase amps to watts calculator</a>
          for a dedicated three-phase tool with line-to-line and line-to-neutral
          options.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 7. REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES ===== -->
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="examples">
    <div class="home-section-heading">
      <h2 id="examples">Real-World Amps to Watts Examples</h2>
      <p>Practical examples of converting amps to watts in common electrical systems.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="home-examples-grid">
      {
        exampleCards.map((card) => (
          <article class={`home-example-card home-card-hover home-tone-${card.tone}`}>
            <span class="home-example-label">{card.label}</span>
            <p class="home-math">{card.formula}</p>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 8. CONVERSION CHART ===== -->
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="conversion-chart">
    <article class="home-reference-card">
      <div class="home-reference-head">
        <h2 id="conversion-chart">Amps to Watts Conversion Chart</h2>
        <p>Quick reference for DC and resistive AC loads (power factor = 1.0).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-table-wrap">
        <table class="home-reference-table home-conversion-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Amps (A)</th>
              <th scope="col">Voltage (V)</th>
              <th scope="col">Watts (W)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              conversionRows.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{row[0]}</td>
                  <td>{row[1]}</td>
                  <td><strong>{row[2]}</strong></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="home-reference-note">
        For AC loads with power factor below 1.0, actual watts will be lower
        than amps × volts. Multiply the result by the equipment's power factor
        (found on the nameplate) to get real power.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 9. AMPS VS WATTS VS VOLTS ===== -->
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="amps-watts-volts">
    <div class="home-section-card">
      <div class="home-section-heading">
        <h2 id="amps-watts-volts">Amps vs Watts vs Volts</h2>
        <p>Understanding the core electrical units used in power calculations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-content-body">
        <div class="home-entity-grid">
          <div class="home-entity-item">
            <strong>Amps (Amperes)</strong>
            <p>Amps measure electrical current — the rate of electron flow through a conductor. Higher amperage means more current, which typically requires larger wire and higher-rated circuit breakers.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="home-entity-item">
            <strong>Volts (Voltage)</strong>
            <p>Volts measure electrical pressure or potential difference — the force that pushes current through a circuit. Common voltages include 12V (batteries), 120V (US outlets), 230V (EU outlets), and 480V (industrial).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="home-entity-item">
            <strong>Watts (Power)</strong>
            <p>Watts measure real electrical power — the rate at which energy is consumed or converted into heat, light, or motion. Watts = Amps × Volts for DC and resistive loads.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="home-entity-item">
            <strong>Power Factor</strong>
            <p>Power factor (PF) indicates how effectively AC power becomes usable real power. It ranges from 0 to 1.0, where 1.0 means all apparent power is real power. Motors and compressors typically have PF between 0.70 and 0.95.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="home-entity-item">
            <strong>Kilowatts (kW)</strong>
            <p>Kilowatts equal watts divided by 1,000. Professional and industrial equipment ratings often use kW because values in the thousands of watts are more practical to express in kilowatts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== 10. WHEN POWER FACTOR MATTERS ===== -->
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="power-factor-matters">
    <div class="home-section-card">
      <div class="home-section-heading">
        <h2 id="power-factor-matters">When Power Factor Matters</h2>
        <p>Not every amps to watts conversion needs power factor.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-content-body">
        <p>
          For purely resistive loads — heaters, incandescent lights, toasters, and
          kettles — power factor is 1.0, and the simple formula <em>Watts = Amps ×
          Volts</em> gives the correct result. Power factor becomes important when
          the load includes inductive components like motors, compressors,
          fluorescent lighting ballasts, and transformers.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are estimating appliance wattage for a household device, you can
          usually ignore power factor. For generator sizing, electrical panel
          planning, and industrial circuit load calculations, always include the
          power factor from the equipment nameplate. Learn more in our
          <a href="/power-factor/">power factor guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

